// No type annotation is needed to reach 00's class — the same-tag,
// different-arity union arises from ordinary inference over two select
// arms. This closes the "you had to write a weird type" escape:
//
//   interp: 7    jit: -1
//
// The union also survives a cross-kernel call:
//   { let g = |v: [`A, `A(i64)]| -> i64 select v { `A => i64:-1, `A(n) => n };
//     g(`A(i64:7)) }   ->  interp 7, jit -1
//
// And the typechecker PROVES both arms are live: dropping either one is
// a compile error ("missing match cases ... `A does not contain
// [`A, `A(i64)]"). So the typechecker requires two arms and marks
// neither dead, while the emitter discriminates them with a test that
// can only see one of the two things that distinguish them.
{ let b = false; let x = select b { true => `A, false => `A(i64:7) }; select x { `A => i64:-1, `A(n) => n } }
